  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nOverview through Early Wednesday Afternoon... \n\nLooking across the region early this morning, the surface high \npressure that has been in place is pushing off to the east. Clear \nskies and relatively calm winds still remain, with most places \ncurrently in the upper 30s to mid 40s. The start of the day on \nWednesday will be fairly calm ahead of our previously mentioned low \npressure system swinging through the Ohio Valley later today.\n\nShowers and Storms w/Possible Severe Potential late Wednesday...\n\nExamining things at the synoptic scale, shortwave energy currently \nover the northern Plains is tracking southeastward at the nose of \nthe polar jet. Additionally, weaker shortwave energy over Texas \nassociated with the subtropical jet is moving east-northeastward \ntowards the Tennessee Valley. The two features will interact and \nphase with each other as the day progresses, promoting cyclogenesis \nthat will produce a deeper surface low over IL/IN/OH by Wednesday \nevening. While the low center will not move directly over our \nforecast area, the strong cold front associated with it will pass \nthrough during the late afternoon and evening hours.\n\nAhead of the low, decent southwesterly flow will bring in a brief \npush of warm air advection and low-level moisture. Highs today will \nbe in the mid to upper 60s, with dew points in the 50s for the \nwestern half of the CWA. While not broadly encompassing, a narrow \nmoisture axis develops ahead of the cold front, with about 0.8-0.9\" \nof PW (80-85th percentile climatologically). From recent REFS \nguidance, this corridor will also have MLCAPE values of 400-600 J/kg \nby peak heating, as well as fairly steep low and mid-level lapse \nrates near 7.0 C. Wind shear will be mostly unidirectional aloft \n(westerly) but quite strong (30-40 kt of 0-6 km bulk shear). \n\nWith the impressive kinematics in place we do expect showers and \nscattered thunderstorms to develop late Wednesday afternoon, moving \neastward across the region. The SPC does have a Marginal Risk for \nsevere weather for places west of I-75 with this system, with the \nprimary concerns being damaging winds and isolated small hail. The \nlimiting factors in play with this includes the limited moisture \nreturn ahead of the low, as well as the timing of the system. Once \nwe lose daytime heating and transition into the overnight, \nconvective inhibition quickly increases and the thermodynamics \ninvolved with this system become less favorable for strong to severe \nstorms.\n\nWhile there is decent likelihood (50-70% chance) in rainfall for the \narea, the faster-moving nature of this system will keep \naccumulations rather low. REFS rainfall amounts from this system are \nin the 0.2-0.35\" range, with LPMM outputting narrow swaths of 0.70-\n0.85\" of rain with the strongest showers/storms. The current timing \nof this event for the area is from 4 PM - 12 AM EST.\n\nReturn of Cold Weather Late Week and Beyond...\n\nOnce the front swings through the area late Wednesday, cold air (-4 \nto -6 C 850 mb temps from latest LREF) surges in behind it. Rapid \npressure rises from the deepening surface low will result in blustery \nnorthwesterly winds into Thursday. Sustained winds will be in the 10-\n15 mph range, with wind gusts as high as 30 mph. While winds will \nstart to diminish some late Thursday, cold air will continue to be \nreinforced due to embedded shortwaves from an upper-level low over \nthe northern Great Lakes region/southern Ontario swinging over us. \nTemperatures on Thursday will be in the upper 40s/low 50s, with \nhighs struggling to get above 50 Friday-Sunday. Overnight \ntemperatures will drop to the coldest we've felt so far this season. \nLows on Friday morning will be in the low to mid 30s, with \nwidespread lows in the 20s Saturday-Tuesday.\n\nIf enough wrap around moisture remains behind the low late Thursday \nthrough Friday, a few rain showers are possible in the eastern half \nof the CWA. With cold overnight temperatures, a few snowflakes \ncannot be ruled out. A few LREF members do pick up on this, so that \nwill be something to monitor as this system develops. Surface high \npressure builds in by the weekend, setting up a dry stretch of \nweather that lasts into early next week.",
